description:
pkix-chain/src/lib.rs:319-322 (verify_chain_default's Errors section):

    /// # Errors
    ///
    /// Returns `Err(Error)` for any validation failure. See [`Error`] in `pkix_path`
    /// and `pkix_revocation` for the full list of failure conditions.

The text says "See [`Error`] in `pkix_path` and `pkix_revocation`." This points at TWO upstream Error types, but the function's actual return is `crate::Result<ValidatedPath>` which uses the local `pkix_chain::Error`. The local Error wraps the upstream errors but ALSO has six other variants (ProfileViolation, OcspDelegation, Aia, PathBuild, AiaDepthExceeded, Identity) that don't exist in either pkix_path or pkix_revocation.

A reader following the cross-reference learns about path/revocation errors only and misses:

- Error::Aia (via NoAiaFetcher's FetchingDisabled when chain is incomplete)
- Error::PathBuild (when the path builder runs out of candidates)
- Error::AiaDepthExceeded (when the AIA recursion cap is hit)

(Error::ProfileViolation, OcspDelegation, and Identity are only produced by the use-case wrappers and verify_chain_default never reaches them, so omitting those is technically fine — but the rustdoc doesn't explain that, so a reader can't tell which omissions are intentional.)

WHY IT MATTERS:

This is the slash-command checklist's:

> Doc comments that describe what the function does at the implementation level rather than what callers should rely on.

Plus the cross-reference is broken: rustdoc renders `[`Error`]` as a link to the local Error symbol (because `Error` resolves first in scope), but the prose says "in pkix_path and pkix_revocation" — which is at best ambiguous and at worst points at types the caller will never see in this function's return.

Fix:

    /// # Errors
    ///
    /// Returns `Err(Error)` for any validation failure. See [`Error`] for the
    /// full set of failure modes; this convenience wrapper can produce any
    /// of `Error::Path`, `Error::Revocation`, or `Error::Aia` (the latter
    /// always as `AiaError::FetchingDisabled` because `NoAiaFetcher` is
    /// hardwired).

PRIORITY 3 because the inaccuracy is a doc-only nit and the reader can find the actual Error variants by visiting the local link. Filed for completeness; flag with the opinion label if you'd prefer not to track this scale of finding.
